    "created_at": "2024-08-01T19:32:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-08-01T19:32:52Z",
    "body": "So I [implemented the rest](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/5750587) of this in Chromium, mainly to see what would be involved. The primary developer-facing changes would be:\r\n\r\n- Calls to `element.parseHTMLUnsafe()` with invalid declarative shadow DOM (see below) would throw an exception.\r\n- Main documents containing invalid declarative shadow DOM would fire an event on `window` while being parsed.\r\n\r\nAt this point, \"invalid declarative shadow DOM\" consists of one of the following:\r\n- invalid host element (e.g. `<progress>`)\r\n- invalid value for `shadowrootmode` (e.g. `<template shadowrootmode=invalid>`)\r\n- more than one `<template shadowrootmode>` within a single host\r\n- declarative shadow root on an element that already has an imperative shadow root\r\n- declarative shadow root on a custom element with disabledFeatures=shadow\r\n\r\nI wanted to get folks thoughts on this behavior. Is this what we want to do? The spec says to do all of this, but no browser does it today. I suspect that's mainly an oversight, and somewhat due to lack of tests. But I'm leaning toward **not** firing exceptions like this, because it would be fairly novel. No other html triggers exceptions in synchronous parsing entry points today. @emilio @annevk ",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
