    "created_at": "2024-08-02T02:33:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-08-02T02:34:18Z",
    "body": "> * Calls to `element.parseHTMLUnsafe()` with invalid declarative shadow DOM (see below) would throw an exception.\r\n\r\nI don't think the spec currently says to do this. The spec always goes straight to reporting to the global `error` event.\r\n\r\nI agree that having `parseHTMLUnsafe()` throw makes more sense. (And any other parser entry points that allow declarative shadow DOM, e.g. probably `Document.parseHTMLUnsafe()`... any others?) But speccing it would take more work than what we currently have.\r\n\r\n> Is this what we want to do?\r\n\r\nThat's a fair question. I guess we now have multiple alternatives:\r\n\r\n- throw an exception for methods / report an exception for parsing that isn't caused by methods\r\n- always report an exception, even for methods\r\n- no observable, testable exceptions, just a console warning/error/etc.\r\n\r\nI don't have strong feelings between these, but the first seems a bit nicer for web developers.\r\n\r\nI'll agenda+ this but of course async discussion is better if we can. Also /cc @smaug----",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
