    "updated_at": "2024-08-02T16:39:54Z",
    "body": "> I don't think the spec currently says to do this. The spec always goes straight to reporting to the global `error` event.\r\n\r\nOk, true. Or... I'll take your word for it. 😄 \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n> That's a fair question. I guess we now have multiple alternatives:\r\n> \r\n> * throw an exception for methods / report an exception for parsing that isn't caused by methods\r\n> * always report an exception, even for methods\r\n> * no observable, testable exceptions, just a console warning/error/etc.\r\n> \r\n> I don't have strong feelings between these, but the first seems a bit nicer for web developers.\r\n\r\nI've been thinking more about this question since yesterday. I think I'm leaning more and more heavily to either option 2 or option 3. HTML parsing has always been \"forgiving\", and that would seem to extend to methods. I.e. `foo.innerHTML=html` never throws, no matter what you stick into `html`. That feels nice in some way, since it mirrors document parsing. It's a different story for XML of course, but that's kind of the point.\r\n\r\nOption 2 feels like the best balance. Nothing throws, so no compat issues or change of philosophy. But it also allows code to deal with problem situations if it wants to, since errors are observable.\r\n\r\nAll of these are weak opinions - I'm happy to go with the consensus. With the one caveat that option <span>#</span>1 might not be web compatible, depending on what exceptions get thrown.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
